Z||000000||85seNUT||05-08-2001||11:27 PM||||Does anyone around the san jose area do porting of the intake manifold?  i hear that it adds a lot of a difference.  the intake manifold is the big orange/red thing that says fiero right?  i dont think i want to do it with a dremel.  well maybe i do.  if no one around he does it does anyone have directions on how to do it.  thanks.  <P>adam||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||05-08-2001||11:33 PM||||Dremmel.... not a good choice of tool. If you try that it MUST be the variable speed one.<P>look for standard abrasives motor spoorts. they have all the stuff and instrucions. There are links in old forum threads. maybe in the archives now.<P>Unless you've done allot of this, DO NOT try to do any thing but a basic port/gasket match and smoothe the rough areas in the head. It is strongly recomended you farm this out or buy a set of parts to practice on. This is a very easy to screw up project. Even a small slip can ruin the part you are working on.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||05-09-2001||08:13 AM||||I used an air die grinder with carbide bits to gasket match port the intake manifold, upper and middle intake plenum, and the exhaust manifolds when I installed my new Grooms long block.  It was very easy with the die grinder.  I think a dremel tool would take forever but it would work.<P>FOCOA magazine did a three part story on doing a 3.1 stroker build-up back around late 1997 that had a good write-up with pictures of how to port the engine including the heads.<P>I'm pretty sure FOCOA still sells back issues.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DXR_DAD||05-09-2001||11:04 AM||||Adam, ---YOU HAVE MAIL---  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||12
Z||000004||CC Rider||05-09-2001||03:25 PM||||Can any one of you that have done the porting before talk a little bit about the benefits you've had once completed||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||05-09-2001||09:08 PM||||The results depend on the level of porting job, and the skill of the person doing the job. No 2 port jobs are identical and if you had the results of someone elses job your's are unlikely to be the same.<P>A basic port matches the castings and gaskets to reduce turbulence. It also smothes the rest of the head openings for the same reason. A basic port job doesn't alter the head passage shape, just smooths them. (Altering there shape imporoperly can hurt performance!) You would only remove rough areas and sharp edges left over from casting the head.<P>A basic job will give you a few HP and maybe a couple FP torque. No huge gains but no major risk of screwing up the performance.<P>A full port would include Major rework of the head passages. This has to be done by an expert and what he will do depends on what the car will be used for. If you botch this you might gain HP but loose lots of torque. (Remember It Torque NOT HP that gets you off the line. HP is more determinates the top speed.)||||reg||1
